WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.528
Passepartout practiced with the Long Noses. The performers wore costumes from the Middle Ages,

2
00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:12.424
and each man had a splendid pair of wings on his shoulders and a very long nose attached to his face.

3
00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:16.692
The noses were made of bamboo and some were ten feet long!

4
00:00:16.693 --> 00:00:20.785
Passepartout was a little embarrassed to be in a circus act again.

5
00:00:20.786 --> 00:00:23.493
It reminded him too much of his youth.

6
00:00:23.494 --> 00:00:28.073
But he cheered up, remembering his long nose was helping him to earn money for food. He

7
00:00:28.074 --> 00:00:32.079
went onstage and took his place with the rest of the performers. They

8
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.271
balanced chairs, balls, and plates on the ends of their long noses. Some

9
00:00:37.272 --> 00:00:43.469
climbed up to stand on their fellow performers' noses, performing handstands and acrobatic flips. The

10
00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.614
theater was full, and the audience was delighted by the spectacle. The

11
00:00:47.615 --> 00:00:51.141
grand finale was a feat called the "Great Pyramid." A

12
00:00:51.142 --> 00:00:55.915
group of acrobats lay on the floor with their noses pointed at the ceiling. A

13
00:00:55.916 --> 00:01:02.283
second group lay on the tips of the noses, and a third group climbed up on the noses of the second group. A

14
00:01:02.284 --> 00:01:06.753
fourth group did the same, and a towering human pyramid was formed. There

15
00:01:06.754 --> 00:01:14.994
was loud applause, and the orchestra began its fanfare when the pyramid began to tip from side to side. One

16
00:01:14.995 --> 00:01:17.744
of the lower noses had vanished! The

17
00:01:17.745 --> 00:01:26.338
pyramid tilted, tumbled, and collapsed like a house of cards. The missing performer was Passepartout. "Master!"

18
00:01:26.339 --> 00:01:31.869
he cried as he jumped into the crowd and fell at the feet of one of the spectators. Mr. Fogg

19
00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:38.492
and Aouda, who were seated in the audience, were shocked—as if they had been struck by lightning. Passepartout was kneeling

20
00:01:38.493 --> 00:01:42.338
before them in his performer's costume. "Passepartout! We are

21
00:01:42.339 --> 00:01:47.610
so happy to find you!" cried Aouda. Mr. Batulcar was

22
00:01:47.611 --> 00:01:56.881
furious. He demanded compensation for his ruined show, and Mr. Fogg happily appeased him with a handful of banknotes. The three traveling

23
00:01:56.882 --> 00:02:00.971
companions left the theater, together once again. As they made

24
00:02:00.972 --> 00:02:07.927
their way through the streets of Yokohama, Aouda explained to Passepartout how they had finally found him. "We reached Yokohama

25
00:02:07.928 --> 00:02:12.114
on the morning of November 14," she said. "And Mr. Fogg

26
00:02:12.115 --> 00:02:17.377
immediately went on board the Carnatic , which was still docked in the harbor. He learned,

27
00:02:17.378 --> 00:02:20.441
to our delight, that you had been onboard." "Yes. Luckily,

28
00:02:20.442 --> 00:02:26.237
I did not miss the steamer," said Passepartout. "So we

29
00:02:26.238 --> 00:02:30.314
knew that you had arrived only the day before," Aouda continued. "We visited the

30
00:02:30.315 --> 00:02:34.757
French and English consuls, but they had received no word of you. After wandering through

31
00:02:34.758 --> 00:02:38.396
the streets, we began to despair. Chance led us

32
00:02:38.397 --> 00:02:41.396
to Mr. Batulcar's theater. We did not

33
00:02:41.397 --> 00:02:46.610
recognize you in your costume, but fortunately, you saw us!" "You cannot imagine,

34
00:02:46.611 --> 00:02:52.510
madam, how happy I am to be back with you!" said Passepartout, smiling widely. He turned to

35
00:02:52.511 --> 00:02:55.399
Mr. Fogg with a serious face. "Sir, please forgive

36
00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.024
my poor behavior," he said. "It was because

37
00:02:59.025 --> 00:03:03.431
of me that you missed the Carnatic . I beg your forgiveness. I was

38
00:03:03.432 --> 00:03:07.453
drunk and did not tell you of the schedule change. It will

39
00:03:07.454 --> 00:03:11.209
never happen again. Please believe me!" "You are

40
00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:15.739
forgiven," said Mr. Fogg. "We will talk no more about it!" They walked

41
00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:21.017
on in silence. Passepartout was relieved to be back with his master. Aouda was

42
00:03:21.018 --> 00:03:24.234
very happy to see her friend once again. Mr. Fogg

43
00:03:24.235 --> 00:03:28.894
was his usual self, as calm and distant as ever. Passepartout then realized

44
00:03:28.895 --> 00:03:37.421
that, although Mr. Fogg and Aouda had missed the Carnatic , somehow they had arrived in Yokohama only one day after him! He turned

45
00:03:37.422 --> 00:03:40.180
to Aouda for an explanation. She described

46
00:03:40.181 --> 00:03:45.802
their search for a boat in the port at Hong Kong and the perilous voyage they had survived. Aouda then

47
00:03:45.803 --> 00:03:50.353
explained what had happened at Shanghai. The Yokohama

48
00:03:50.354 --> 00:03:55.294
steamer was already underway as they approached the harbor. At Mr.

49
00:03:55.295 --> 00:04:00.899
Fogg's request, the Tankadere signaled the steamer with its whistle. The captain

50
00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:08.021
of the steamer heard the signal and saw the Tankadere 's flag at half-mast, which indicates a ship in distress. Mr.

51
00:04:08.022 --> 00:04:13.627
Fogg had arranged this. The steamer directed its course to the little boat. Phileas

52
00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:20.447
Fogg quickly paid Captain Bunsby their agreed price, rewarding him with an additional 550 pounds. The

53
00:04:20.448 --> 00:04:26.593
steamer let down a ladder, and Mr. Fogg and Aouda climbed aboard, followed by Mr. Fix. Mr.

54
00:04:26.594 --> 00:04:33.274
Fogg listened politely as Aouda spoke, without adding a word of his own. She finished

55
00:04:33.275 --> 00:04:40.059
her story as they reached the steamer, which was now docked in Yokohama harbor and bound for San Francisco. Mr. Fogg

56
00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:46.342
then gave Passepartout the necessary funds to buy clothes that better suited his position. Within an

57
00:04:46.343 --> 00:04:58.956
hour Passepartout had cut off his long nose, parted with his wings, and looked like his master's servant once more. Passepartout's Guidebook

58
00:04:58.957 --> 00:05:02.235
That was a neat little trick that Mr. Fogg arranged, wasn't it?

59
00:05:02.236 --> 00:05:08.465
Putting the ship's flag at half-mast, I mean. In the days before radio, how did ships communicate?

60
00:05:08.466 --> 00:05:13.364
Did they yell really loudly? Ha-ha! No, they used flags.

61
00:05:13.365 --> 00:05:18.840
Flags are very important at sea. They can mean the difference between life and death!

62
00:05:18.841 --> 00:05:25.519
Flags are flown when a ship is entering, or leaving, a harbor, and when a ship is sailing in foreign waters.

63
00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.316
But why did the ship, the Tankadere , fly its flag only halfway up the

64
00:05:30.317 --> 00:05:36.021
mast? Was the flag broken? No no! Flying a flag halfway like that is called "half-mast."

65
00:05:36.022 --> 00:05:38.551
It means the ship is in trouble.

66
00:05:38.552 --> 00:05:43.270
On land a flag at half-mast means that someone important has died.

67
00:05:43.271 --> 00:05:47.637
There are special days when a country's flag is flown at half-mast too.

68
00:05:47.638 --> 00:05:49.630
Why did this tradition develop?

69
00:05:49.631 --> 00:05:56.590
Well, people decided that if a flag is halfway down, there is room for another "invisible" flag at the top of the flagpole.

70
00:05:56.591 --> 00:06:04.438
This "invisible" flag represents and honors the person who has died.

